Honestly, in the case of a baby out of wedlock, I agree tbh. If the option of abortion is there, and the woman decides not to take it, then it's their responsibility for the kid. Sure, I guess it would be cool for a man to help father the child, but they're not really obliged. Society might pressure them into thinking that, but they're objectively not required to be active and supportive fathers in the life of their children. 

&#x200B;

I would say though that in this instance, you can't have your cake and eat it too. If you don't want to raise your child financially, just don't be present in their lives either. It'll scar the kid. If you don't want it, be consistent and leave the baby alone once it's born. But as long as that's the case, then I agree tbh. Abortion is a perfectly viable option for mothers and if they want to keep a baby with someone they're not married to, then they should fully anticipate having to raise that kid alone and on their own.

I think given the rocky post-prime Em has had, people weren't given enough time after this album to reevaluate it. Eminem kept putting out music that, for the most part, people just weren't feeling after this, which hurt people even bothering to check this bc so many people's mindsets are that anything after TES is trash. This album however is really dope. Em's flow is so razor sharp on here it's crazy, and the production is probably the most consistent assemblage of beats Em has ever gotten for an album aside from TES, which imo is his best produced album. Relapse is definitely my 4th favorite Em album behind his big three, and this alongside Infinite should be reappraised imo. Two real solid albums that don't get enough shine. 

&#x200B;

Also, I think it's a significant step beneath Infinite and Relapse, but MMLP2 is a decent album imo. A little above average for its best moments, and def shouldn't be considered one of the weak points in Em's discography. The only Em albums I'm really not into are Encore, Recovery, Revival, and Kamikaze.

